e React versions.","evidence":["Found 139 test files","Test framework configured","End-to-end tests configured","Coverage tracking configured","High test-to-source ratio (1.05)"],"strengths":["139 test files with a 1.05 test-to-source ratio","Playwright e2e suite covering suspense, concurrent transitions, mutate/server-actions, and dev/prod build modes","Coverage tracking configured","Recent dedicated investment in e2e restructuring (#4289) and compatibility test workflows for React 17/main/canary"],"gaps":["No evidence of mutation testing to validate the test suite's own effectiveness","No accessibility (axe/pa11y) or performance/load (Lighthouse CI/k6) smoke testing found","No formal contract/API-schema testing tooling, though less applicable to a client-side hook library"]},{"id":"D3","name":"CI/CD & Delivery","weight":0.14,"score":76,"signalScore":75,"llmScore":76,"summary":"CI runs on 5 GitHub Actions workflows that gate merges with lint, build, unit test, type-check, and a matrix e2e job (dev/prod modes, containerized Playwright), plus scheduled compatibility runs against React 17 (legacy) and React canary, and a dedicated OIDC-based trusted-publishing release workflow that avoids long-lived npm tokens - a solid, multi-stage pipeline for a client library, though it has no declarative infra/GitOps layer since none is architecturally needed here.","evidence":["GitHub Actions CI present","Multiple CI workflows (5)","CI runs tests","CI runs linting","CI runs a build"],"strengths":["5 workflows covering lint/build/test/type-check/e2e, gating on push and PR to main","Scheduled compatibility testing against React canary and legacy React 17","OIDC trusted publishing removes long-lived npm publish credentials","Matrix e2e testing across dev/prod build modes with artifact upload on failure"],"gaps":["No IaC/GitOps/policy-as-code (expected for a library, but means no declarative deploy path exists to evaluate)","Release workflow relies on manual dispatch and string-parsing of package.json's version rather than an automated versioning tool like changesets","Branch-protection signals show required approvals and signatures but not required status checks","No progressive-delivery or feature-flag mechanism, though not clearly applicable to a published library"]},{"id":"D4","name":"Agentic Workflows","weight":0.12,"score":1,"signalScore":0,"llmScore":2,"summary":"No agentic workflows were found anywhere in the repository or CI: no AI code-review bots, no LLM invocations inside CI, no auto-fix/auto-PR tooling, and no Dependabot/Renovate automation of any kind - corroborated by the process signal showing 0% of sampled PRs had any AI involvement. AI today plays no visible role in the actual shipping loop.","evidence":["No agentic/AI-in-CI workflows detected (e.g. AI review bots, LLM steps in CI, auto-merge)"],"strengths":["The existing modular workflow structure (composite 'install' action, separate test/e2e/release jobs) would make it straightforward to slot in an AI review or auto-fix step later"],"gaps":["No AI PR-review bot configured (CodeRabbit, Claude Code review action, Greptile, Sweep, etc.)","No LLM steps inside CI","No Dependabot/Renovate or other automated dependency-update tooling, let alone auto-merge","0% of analyzed PRs show any AI involvement"]},{"id":"D5","name":"Documentation & Knowledge","weight":0.09,"score":46,"signalScore":45,"llmScore":47,"summary":"Documentation basics are in place - a substantial, well-structured README, a CONTRIBUTING.md, and an examples/ directory with runnable Next.js sample apps - but the repo defers most in-depth documentation to an external site (swr.vercel.app) rather than an in-repo docs/ or ADR trail, and no CHANGELOG.md was identified in the sampled evidence, so an agent working purely from this repository has a thinner map of 'why' than a human following the external docs link.","evidence":["Substantial README (3737 chars, 4 sections)","CONTRIBUTING.md","Examples directory"],"strengths":["Substantial README (3737 chars, 4 sections) with a working quick-start example","CONTRIBUTING.md present","examples/ directory with multiple runnable sample apps","External docs site linked prominently from the README"],"gaps":["No in-repo docs/ or ADR trail (docs/adr, decisions/) found","No CHANGELOG.md detected in sampled evidence","Core documentation lives outside the repo, reducing what's directly available to an agent reading only this codebase","No typedoc/API-doc generation evidenced for the public TypeScript API"]},{"id":"D6","name":"Code Quality & Guardrails","weight":0.07,"score":77,"signalScore":76,"llmScore":77,"summary":"Code-quality guardrails are enforced rather than merely present: TypeScript strict mode is on (confirmed directly in tsconfig.json), a formatter/linter pair (oxfmt/oxlint, adopted recently) runs in CI's 'run-all-checks' step, CODEOWNERS restricts the whole tree to two maintainers, and the default branch requires a PR with at least one approval plus code-owner review and signed commits.","evidence":["Formatter configured","TypeScript strict mode","Pre-commit hooks","CODEOWNERS","PR review coverage 60% (15 merged · 73% small · 0% reverted)","Pull requests required to merge (1 approval(s) + code owners)"],"strengths":["TypeScript strict mode enabled with added strictness flags (noUnusedLocals, noUnusedParameters, strictBindCallApply)","Formatter/linter (oxfmt/oxlint) enforced in CI","CODEOWNERS + required PR review + signed commits on the default branch","Pre-commit hooks configured"],"gaps":["PR review coverage is only 60% of merged PRs, averaging 0.7 reviews per PR","Required status checks are not confirmed enabled on the branch-protection ruleset, only approval/code-owner/signature requirements","All review authority is concentrated in 2 codeowners - a single-point-of-failure risk for consistent enforcement","No commitlint/conventional-commit enforcement config confirmed, despite 63% informal adherence"]},{"id":"D7","name":"Commit & Velocity Signals","weight":0.07,"score":80,"signalScore":80,"llmScore":80,"summary":"Commit history shows an actively maintained, disciplined project: 63% conventional-commit-style messages, regular cadence, a push within the last 30 days, and at least one AI-assisted commit trailer - though at just 1 of 30 recent commits, AI-attributed authorship is a minor fraction of overall velocity rather than a systemic pattern, and merge velocity (0.7h median) far outpaces first-review turnaround (18h median).","evidence":["30 recent commits analyzed","AI-assisted commits present (1 of 30)","Conventional commit style (63%)","Active commit cadence","Actively maintained (pushed within 30 days)"],"strengths":["63% conventional-commit adherence","Actively maintained - pushed within 30 days, regular cadence","At least one AI-assisted commit found in recent history","73% of PRs are small (≤200 line changes), supporting fast, reviewable batches"],"gaps":["Only 1 of 30 recent commits shows AI co-authorship - AI-assisted development isn't yet a visible, repeated pattern","Median time-to-merge (0.7h) far outpaces median time-to-first-review (18h), implying many merges may precede a completed review","No evidence of bot-authored commits (e.g. automated formatting or dependency-bump bots)"]},{"id":"D8","name":"AI Process & Harness","weight":0.12,"score":23,"signalScore":21,"llmScore":24,"summary":"Beyond structured issue templates and a well-configured, signed-commit, code-owner-gated branch-protection ruleset, there is no dedicated AI-process harness: no evals/golden-test directory for LLM output and no prompt or agent-spec library (no .claude/agents/, no prompts/). Notably, the confirmed CONTRIBUTING.md (used as a D5 signal) isn't reflected in this dimension's detected signals despite the rubric explicitly counting CONTRIBUTING guidance toward a 'structured contribution process.'","evidence":["Structured issue templates","Default branch protected (7 rules) (signed commits)"],"strengths":["Structured issue templates for bug reports/feature requests","Default branch protected with 7 ruleset rules including signed commits","CONTRIBUTING.md documents contributor process, though not yet leveraged as agent-facing guidance"],"gaps":["No evals/golden-test setup to verify AI-generated output distinctly from human output","No prompt or agent-spec library (.claude/agents/, prompts/)","No Definition-of-Done or agent-specific contribution guidance","Any AI-produced changes route through the same generic review path with no distinguishing verification step"]},{"id":"D9","name":"Supply Chain & Security","weight":0.09,"score":40,"signalScore":40,"llmScore":40,"summary":"Security posture is fixed at 40/100 by the deterministic check battery: strong marks on branch protection (8/10 - required approval, code-owner review, signed commits), dangerous-workflow hygiene (10/10 - no script-injection or untrusted-checkout patterns), and a documented SECURITY.md policy (10/10), but zeros across nearly every supply-chain-as-code control - no SAST wired into CI, no committed dependency-update tool, 0 of 8 Action references pinned by SHA/digest, no signed releases/provenance, and no SBOM generation. Exposure from known vulnerable dependencies could not be assessed (no lockfile/alert access).","evidence":["Branch protection [posture/high]: 8/10 — Default branch: protected, 1 approval(s), code-owner review, signed commits.","Dangerous workflow [posture/critical]: 10/10 — No script-injection or untrusted-checkout patterns detected.","Token permissions [posture/high]: 2/10 — 1/5 workflows set an explicit `permissions:` scope.","SAST [posture/medium]: 0/10 — No SAST (CodeQL/Semgrep/Sonar) wired into CI.","Dependency updates [posture/high]: 0/10 — No dependency-update tool committed.","Pinned dependencies [posture/medium]: 0/10 — 0/8 Actions/images pinned by SHA/digest (8 action refs).","Security policy [posture/medium]: 10/10 — SECURITY.md documents a vulnerability-reporting path.","Signed releases [posture/high]: 0/10 — No artifact signing / provenance in CI.","SBOM [posture/low]: 0/10 — No SBOM generation.","Known vulnerabilities [exposure/high]: n/a/10 — Dependency vulnerabilities not inspected (no lockfile / no alert access)."],"strengths":["Branch protection is strong: required approval, code-owner review, signed commits (8/10)","No dangerous workflow patterns (script injection, untrusted checkout) detected (10/10)","SECURITY.md documents a vulnerability-reporting path (10/10)","OIDC trusted publishing (seen in trigger-release.yml) removes long-lived npm credentials from the release path"],"gaps":["Add CodeQL (or Semgrep) scanning that runs on pull_request.","Add a `.github/dependabot.yml` (or Renovate) config.","Pin third-party Actions to a full commit SHA (and container images to a digest).","Sign releases with cosign and emit SLSA provenance (actions/attest).","Generate an SBOM (Syft/CycloneDX) on release.","Add a least-privilege top-level `permissions:` block (default `contents: read`) to every workflow."]}],"headline":"SWR shows strong, enforced engineering fundamentals - deep test coverage, gated CI, real code-quality guardrails - typical of a mature OSS project, but almost no AI-native tooling, governance, or agentic-workflow adoption; AI use here, if it happens, is informal and invisible rather than a shared team practice.","strengths":["Exceptional test discipline: 139 test files, a 1.05 test-to-source ratio, and a Playwright e2e suite covering suspense, transitions, and dev/prod build modes across multiple React versions","Enforced CI/CD gates: 5 workflows run lint, build, type-check, unit, and e2e tests on every PR, plus scheduled compatibility runs against React 17 and React canary","Real code-quality enforcement: TypeScript strict mode, CODEOWNERS, a formatter/linter in CI, and a branch-protection ruleset requiring approval, code-owner review, and signed commits","Modern, low-risk release mechanics: OIDC trusted publishing to npm removes long-lived publish credentials from the pipeline","Active, disciplined maintenance cadence: 63% conventional commits, regular pushes, fast merge turnaround"],"risks":["No machine-readable AI guidance exists anywhere (CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md absent) - any AI assistant working on this repo starts from zero shared context every time","AI has no visible role in the actual delivery loop: 0% of sampled PRs show AI involvement, no AI review bots, no LLM-in-CI steps, so there's no repeatable way to trust or scale AI contributions","Supply-chain controls beyond branch protection are largely absent: no SAST, no dependency-update automation, no pinned Action SHAs, no signed/attested releases - a gap that matters more, not less, as automated changes increase in volume","Review depth is thin relative to merge speed (60% reviewed rate, 0.7 avg reviews/PR, 0.7h median merge time) and concentrated on 2 codeowners - a bus-factor and consistency risk","No AI-specific verification harness (no evals, no prompt/agent library) means there's currently no way to check AI-produced changes against a distinct quality bar before human review"],"roadmap":[{"title":"Agents currently have no shared map of this repo","dimension":"D1","impact":"high","effort":"low","rationale":"Without a CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md, every AI session has to rediscover the pnpm build/test/e2e commands, the multi-export module layout, and dev/prod e2e conventions from scratch - knowledge that already lives in CONTRIBUTING.md and the workflow files but isn't packaged for an agent to consume directly.","explore":["What would it look like to capture the pnpm build/test/e2e commands and the dual dev/prod e2e modes in a single agent-readable file?","Is there tribal knowledge among the two codeowners (react-server export gotchas, suspense edge cases) that would help an agent avoid recently-fixed mistakes?"],"levelUnlock":"L2->L3"},{"title":"AI involvement in PRs is currently invisible or near-zero","dimension":"D4","impact":"medium","effort":"medium","rationale":"With 0% of PRs showing AI involvement and no AI review bots or LLM steps in CI, any AI assistance today is informal and untracked - there's no way to see whether AI-authored changes get scrutiny comparable to human ones, and no compounding automation like auto-fix or auto-triage exists yet.","explore":["Would it help the team to know which PRs had AI assistance, even informally, so review effort can be calibrated accordingly?","Is there appetite for trying an AI review bot on a lower-stakes subset of PRs (e.g. examples/ changes) before wider adoption?"],"levelUnlock":"L2->L3"},{"title":"Review depth doesn't fully keep pace with merge velocity","dimension":"D6","impact":"medium","effort":"low","rationale":"A 0.7h median time-to-merge against an 18h median time-to-first-review, with a 60% reviewed rate and 0.7 avg reviews/PR concentrated on two codeowners, means a growing share of AI-accelerated contributions could land with less scrutiny just as change volume rises - worth understanding before leaning further into AI-authored PRs.","explore":["Does the current review rate reflect deliberate trust in small, low-risk PRs (73% are small), or would the team want a firmer gate as AI-generated PR volume grows?","Would a lightweight second-look, even an automated one, help distribute review load beyond the two codeowners?"],"levelUnlock":"L3->L4"},{"title":"Supply-chain guardrails beyond branch protection are largely unexplored","dimension":"D9","impact":"high","effort":"medium","rationale":"Branch protection and a SECURITY.md are strong, but there's no SAST, no automated dependency updates, no pinned action SHAs, and no signed/attested releases - as AI agents start proposing or even merging changes, these controls are what let humans trust that an agent-authored dependency bump or workflow edit doesn't quietly introduce a supply-chain risk.","explore":["Would pinning the 8 GitHub Actions currently referenced by version tag to SHAs